# Audit Flow — dwic

> A design auditor that runs inside Claude Code. Install an MCP server or run one CLI command to get machine-checkable findings on color contrast, typography, spacing, accessibility, forms, navigation, motion, and copy — plus a browser companion that renders Claude's design outputs live. For code-first designers and engineers who use Claude Code.

Generated by gist • May 2026 (mirrors `/llms.txt`; canonical `.gist` for dwic)

---

## Product Overview

- What it is: An MCP server (`@imrandwc/dwic` on npm, currently `1.0.0-alpha.6`) that adds 13 design specialists to Claude Code, plus a zero-friction CLI audit (`npx @imrandwc/dwic audit` — also `--watch` for live re-audit on file change) that scans a codebase and prints an 8-category design-system report. Audit findings cite related AI UX patterns from aiuxdesign.guide — a real integration backed by `src/data/aiex-pattern-map.ts`, not a marketing claim. A browser companion at designwithclaude.com persists project profile, mints install tokens, and hosts `/how-to-design-with-claude` as the cornerstone workflow guide.
- Who it's for: Code-first designers and design-engineering-inclined engineers who already use Claude Code. Teams shipping a Next.js / Vite / Tailwind / shadcn app who want to catch contrast fails, token drift, off-grid spacing, and missing reduced-motion guards during development — not in a separate design-review ritual.
- AI approach: Tool layer, not canvas. dwic does not generate prototypes or screens. It audits what's already in the repo and assists Claude Code while it ships — preserving a mandated accent, enforcing a base spacing unit, surfacing missing `aria-describedby` wiring, flagging `transition: all`. Claude Design handles the canvas; dwic handles the rigor.

---

## Audit Flow (CLI)

### Intent

- Goal: Give a designer or engineer a single command that takes under 10 seconds to run, outputs a screenshot-worthy terminal dashboard, writes a shareable markdown report, and returns a CI-usable exit code — with zero install, zero account, zero config.
- User: A developer who just heard about dwic. They are skeptical that "AI design tool" means anything. They will give it one shot from the root of their project. If the first run is noisy or silent or wrong, they close the tab.
- Core anxiety: "I'll run this and it'll be slop — either false positives on framework CSS I didn't write, or clean-greens that miss the real problems I can see with my eyes."
- Not trying to: Generate a design system. That's the specialists' job, inside Claude Code, with a brief. The CLI never emits fresh tokens.
- Not trying to: Lint code style (Prettier, ESLint, Stylelint already do this). dwic audits design-system decisions — tokens, contrast, motion, copy rigor — not syntax.
- Not trying to: Block commits by default. Exit code mirrors severity so teams can opt into CI gating; it never installs a hook or rewrites files.
- Not trying to: Run on every save. This is an on-demand audit, not a watcher. Noise compounds if it runs continuously.

### Interaction Model

**Primary flow:**
1. User runs `npx @imrandwc/dwic audit` at the root of a Next.js / Vite / Astro / Remix / SvelteKit / Expo project. First-run prints a one-shot opt-out notice about anonymous telemetry (category counts + framework label; no PII, no file contents). The notice is stored in `~/.dwic/state.json` and never repeats.
2. The walker traverses cwd, skipping `node_modules`, `.next`, `dist`, `.git`, `.dwic`, `coverage`, `build`. It collects `.css` files (sorting `themes.css` / `tokens.css` ahead of plain CSS so token parsers see the canonical surface first) and JSX/TSX/HTML files pre-filtered to those with an opening element (so hooks and utils don't pollute the a11y audit).
3. Eight audit categories run in parallel off the walked inputs: color, typography, spacing, accessibility, form, navigation, motion, copy. Each returns typed `Finding[]` with severity `error | warn | info`. `form` is skipped if there's no form markup, `navigation` if no `<nav>`, `motion` if no transition/animation declared — a skip is neither clean nor a finding, it's absence.
4. The dashboard renders: one row per category (severity icons ✗ / ⚠ / ·, severity-sorted rows, clean categories last), a totals summary, up to 3 MCP follow-up suggestions grounded in the actual errors/warns (e.g. "Run `color-specialist` to fix the 2 contrast fails"), and an install CTA. Hand-rolled ANSI, respects `NO_COLOR` and non-TTY.
5. A markdown report is written to `.dwic/audit-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md` with full findings grouped by severity per category. The Next Steps section only mentions tools for non-clean categories. No absolute paths in the content — the report is meant to be shared in a PR, a Slack, or a screenshot.
6. Process exits with code 0 (clean), 1 (warns only), or 2 (any error). CI uses the exit code; humans read the dashboard.

**Key interactions:**
- One command, no flags required: `npx @imrandwc/dwic audit` is the happy path. Flags (`--json`, `--cwd=`, `--max-files=N`, `--css=`, `--markup=`, `--no-telemetry`) exist for CI and power users — never for first-run.
- No config file. Ever. Config drift is the first thing a skeptical developer uses to write a tool off. dwic has sensible defaults (200-file cap, skip-set, prioritised CSS) and no `.dwicrc`.
- Telemetry is opt-out, first-run-visible, PII-free, and always overridable by `--no-telemetry` or `DWIC_TELEMETRY=off`. The payload is schema-versioned (`dwic.audit.summary/1`) so future versions can coexist.
- Findings cite evidence, not verdicts. Each finding includes the token name, the computed contrast ratio, the off-grid px value, or the offending selector — not a generic "violates best practice."

**When things go wrong:**
- The project has no design tokens at all: the color/typography/spacing audits return `info`-severity "no tokens detected" rather than faking a clean pass. An empty system is a signal, not a win.
- The project has framework-generated CSS (Tailwind preflight, shadcn globals): the walker prioritises the designer's canonical files (`themes.css`, `tokens.css`) so findings are grounded in the hand-authored surface. Framework CSS is read but sorted last; duplicate tokens from two sources flag as a consistency finding, not noise.
- The command is run from a non-project directory: the walker returns no files, the dashboard prints a "nothing to audit" empty state with a one-line hint, exit code 0. No crash, no trace.
- The telemetry endpoint is down: 2-second timeout, silent failure. The audit never blocks on a network call.

### Design Decisions

**CLI audit before MCP install**
- Chose: A public `npx @imrandwc/dwic audit` that needs no token, no account, no config — runs cold against any project.
- Over: Gating every dwic capability behind the token-and-install funnel (`/start` → mint token → `npx ... setup --token=...`).
- Because: The install funnel works for designers who were already convinced. Skeptics — the larger audience — need a 30-second proof that the tool sees real problems in their real code. The CLI is the screenshot-worthy moment that converts a tweet into a trial. It also gives us a CLI → MCP conversion metric: any `clientId` that later fires `__mcp.connected__` came from the CLI-first funnel.

**Pure functions under the tool wrappers**
- Chose: Every audit category is a pure function (`runColorAudit`, `runMotionAudit`, etc.) that takes walked input and returns typed findings. The CLI and the MCP tools both call the same functions.
- Over: Duplicating audit logic in the CLI to keep it dependency-light, or shelling out to the MCP server from the CLI.
- Because: The MCP server already had these audits factored as pure helpers (the whole point of batches 2 and 3). Sharing the audit brain means the CLI dashboard and the MCP tool findings can never disagree — they're the same code, reached by different entry points.

**Severity-sorted dashboard, errors first**
- Chose: Dashboard rows sort errors first, warns next, clean last. Summary totals line below. Up to 3 MCP follow-up suggestions grounded in actual findings.
- Over: Alphabetical categories, grouped-by-severity sections, or collapsible tree output.
- Because: The dashboard is the single frame a developer will screenshot and share. The most damaging information has to be at the top of the screenshot, not below a scroll. Alphabetical order hides severity. Tree output optimises for completeness over scannability — wrong trade-off for a terminal artifact.

**No `chalk` dependency, hand-rolled ANSI**
- Chose: A ~30-line ANSI helper module with `NO_COLOR` and non-TTY detection.
- Over: `chalk`, `picocolors`, or any color dependency.
- Because: `npx`-installed CLIs pay for every dependency in cold-start latency. The audit is supposed to feel instant. Also: `chalk`'s v5-ESM-only migration has broken enough projects that keeping dwic dep-free is a trust signal. The hand-rolled helper is 30 lines we fully understand.

**Telemetry as schema-versioned payload, PII-free**
- Chose: `dwic.audit.summary/1` payload with framework label + category counts + totals + anonymous client id. First-run opt-out notice, `--no-telemetry`, `DWIC_TELEMETRY=off`.
- Over: No telemetry at all (we'd learn nothing), or richer telemetry (file paths, finding content — would spook users).
- Because: Post-launch we need to know whether the CLI funnel converts to MCP installs. A versioned, PII-free payload is the minimum signal that answers the question without asking users to feel watched. The opt-out is one-shot-visible so it's informed, not buried in a privacy policy.

**Exit code mirrors severity**
- Chose: 0 (clean), 1 (warns only), 2 (any error).
- Over: Always exit 0 (friendlier to local runs), or always exit nonzero on findings.
- Because: Teams that want a CI gate can add one line to their workflow. Teams that don't won't notice — the exit code is invisible in interactive terminals. Mapping to three codes instead of two lets warn-tolerant teams gate on errors only.

### Patterns Used

- Progressive Disclosure: The CLI defaults to a dashboard + report; the `--json` flag surfaces the same data as a machine-readable schema. Power users discover it via `--help`. Beginners never see it. → https://aiuxdesign.guide/patterns/progressive-disclosure
- Graceful Degradation: `NO_COLOR` env var, non-TTY detection, telemetry-off, and `--cwd=` all cover constrained environments (CI, CI logs, air-gapped networks, reviewers who just want piped output). No path is color-only or network-only. → https://aiuxdesign.guide/patterns/graceful-degradation
- Error Recovery: The markdown report persists at `.dwic/audit-<date>.md` so re-runs don't destroy history. Failed telemetry posts never block exit. A mid-audit crash on one category does not take down the other seven. → https://aiuxdesign.guide/patterns/error-recovery

### Constraints

- Cold-start latency: The CLI runs via `npx`, which means the first run downloads the package. Every extra dependency adds seconds. We intentionally keep the runtime dependency set minimal (MCP SDK + zod for the server, zero runtime deps for the CLI beyond Node built-ins) so the first audit feels instant.
- Public package, private token: The same npm package ships both the token-free CLI and the token-gated MCP server. The CLI must never read, expect, or reference a token. The MCP server must never fail open if a token is required. One binary, two contracts — enforced by tests.
- Honest findings or no findings: Every audit category has an explicit "skip" condition. Returning a clean pass when there was nothing to audit is worse than returning nothing. `form` is skipped if no `<form>` tag was found, `motion` if no transitions exist. A skip is rendered differently from a clean pass in the dashboard.
- No file writes outside `.dwic/`: The CLI writes only to `.dwic/audit-<date>.md` in cwd and `~/.dwic/state.json` for telemetry opt-out state. It never edits source files, never adds lockfile entries, never touches `.git`. Any future auto-fix feature requires an explicit opt-in flag and a dry-run default.

### Not This

- This is not a canvas tool. dwic does not generate screens, prototypes, Figma frames, or design mockups. Anthropic's Claude Design handles the canvas surface. dwic is the layer underneath — auditing the system that canvas outputs will eventually render against.
- This is not a linter. Prettier, ESLint, Stylelint handle code style. dwic audits design-system decisions: token drift, contrast ratios, base spacing unit, motion guards, copy rigor. There is no overlap with code style; findings never cite indentation, semicolons, or import order.
- This is not a generic "AI design assistant." It does not have a chat interface, a prompt box, or a "describe what you want" flow. The CLI is zero-prompt: it reads your code and reports findings. The MCP specialists take structured inputs (a brief, an existing palette, a mandated accent) and return structured outputs.
- This is not competing with 45-skills slash-command libraries. dwic's V1 markdown-plugin library lives at `/library` as a free complement — role-based prompts for Claude Code slash commands. The V2 MCP product is a different shape: server-side audit logic, structured outputs, persistent memory across calls.
- Unlike Figma plugins or Sketch inspectors, dwic never opens a GUI. It's CLI + Claude Code + browser companion — three surfaces, all text-driven or structured-output-driven. There is no canvas, no inspector panel, no drag handles.
- Unlike tools that rebrand every quarter, the name is a construction, not a word. dwic = d + wi + c = design with claude. Pronounced "dwick." The domain stays `designwithclaude.com` as a long-form brand explainer — every shared URL teaches the expansion. The short brand name and long-form domain are intentional and both stay.

### Open Questions

- Whether the CLI should gain an `--fix` flag for mechanical findings (adding `prefers-reduced-motion` guards, replacing `transition: all` with an explicit property list). Arguments for: shortens the loop for trivial fixes. Arguments against: once dwic writes source files, the trust bar goes up 10x and any false positive becomes a bug report about "dwic broke my CSS." Leaning toward: defer until audit coverage stabilises and we have telemetry on which findings users actually fix.
- Whether the MCP specialists should auto-run audit mode when they detect existing tokens in the project (via `set-project-profile` + auto-detect), rather than requiring an explicit `mode: "audit"` input. Arguments for: designers shouldn't have to choose between "generate" and "audit" — the tool should know. Arguments against: implicit mode switching is the root of most "why did it do that?" bug reports.
- How to measure whether the CLI → MCP conversion funnel is actually working. Current plan: watch unique `clientId`s hit `__cli.audit.summary__`, then check whether those same ids later fire `__mcp.connected__`. Open: what ratio signals "the CLI works" vs "the CLI is a standalone product, not a funnel"? The Day-7 decision checkpoint in PROGRESS.md will use this data to decide whether to double down on the auditor or reassess the 45-skills library framing.
